  "response": "Margarita R. Martín | ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/author/24402918400/margarita-r-martin)\nciteturn67search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Purification and properties of two succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenases from Klebsiella pneumoniae\n  * Research article Abstract only\n\nPurification and properties of two succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenases from Klebsiella pneumoniae\n\nM. Sànchez, … M.R. Martín, … A. Garrido-Pertierra\n\nBiochimica Et Biophysica Acta General Subjects • Volume 990, Issue 3 • 24 March 1989\n\n    * View abstract\n\nTwo forms of succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase have been isolated in Klebsiella pneumoniae M5a1. The two enzymes could be separated by filtration on Sephacryl S-300 and their apparent molecular weights were approx. 275 000 and 300000. The large enzyme is specific for NADP. The smaller enzyme, which is induced by growth on 3-hydroxyphenylacetic acid, 4-hydroxyphenylacetic acid, 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetic acid and γ-aminoburyrate, has been purified to 96% homogeneity by affinity chromatography. The NAD-linked succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase was able to use NADP as cofactor. Its induction is coordinated with 3- and 4-hydroxylase, the enzymes which initiate degradation of 3- and 4-hydroxyphenylactic acid. The NAD-linked form is also induced by exogenous succinic semialdehyde. The large enzyme is specific for NADP and has been isolated from a defective mutant which lacked the activity of the NAD-linked succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase. Activity and stability conditions and true K_{m} values for substrates and cosubstrates of the two enzymes were determined. Some aspects of the induction of the NAD-linked enzyme participating in the metabolism of 4-hydroxyphenylacetic and γ-aminobutyrate were studied.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCatabolism of 3- and 4-hydroxyphenylacetic acid by Klebsiella pneumoniae (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/21303788_Catabolism_of_3-_and_4-hydroxyphenylacetic_acid_by_Klebsiella_pneumoniae)\nciteturn67search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 35.6 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; SANCHEZ, ... Purification and properties of two succinic\n\nR.\n\n&\n\nPOSTGATE, J.\n\nR.\n\n(1987).\n\nA\n\nrapid and\n\nefficient method for plasmid transformation of\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae\n\nand\n\nEscherichia coli. Journal\n\nof\n\nGeneral\n\nMicrobiology\n\n133,2053-2057.\n\nMILLER, J. H. (1972).\n\nExperiments in Molecular Genetics\n\npp. 135-139.\n\nCold Spring Harbor, NY: Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory.\n\nPARKE, D.\n\n&\n\nORNSTON,\n\nL.\n\nN. (1976). Constitutive synthesis\n\nof\n\nenzymes\n\nof\n\nthe protocatechuate pathway and of the /3-ketoadipate uptake\n\nsystem in mutant strains of\n\nPsFdomonas putida. Journal\n\nof\n\nBacteriology\n\n126,\n\n272-28 1.\n\nPOSTMA, P. W. (1986). Catabolite repression and related processes.\n\nSymposia\n\nof\n\nthe Society\n\nfor\n\nGeneral Microbiology\n\n39, 21-49.\n\nSANCHEZ,\n\nM., FERNANDEZ, J., MART~N, M., GIBELLO, A.\n\n&\n\nGARRIDO-\n\nPERTIERRA,\n\nA.\n\n(1989). Purification and properties of two succinic\n\nsemialdehyde dehydrogenases from\n\nKlebsiella pneumoniae. Biochi-\n\nmica et Siophysica Acta\n\n990,\n\n225-23 1.\n\nSPARNINS,\n\nV.\n\nL.\n\n&\n\nCHAPMAN,\n\nP.\n\nJ. (1976). Catabolism\n\nof\n\nL-tyrosine by\n\nthe homoprotocatechuate pathway in Gram-positive bacteria.\n\nJournal\n\nof\n\nBacteriology\n\n127, 362-366.\n\nSPARNINS,\n\nV.\n\nL.,\n\nCHAPMAN, P. J.\n\n&\n\nDAGLEY,\n\nS.\n\n(1974). Bacterial\n\ndegradation of 4-hydroxyphenylacetic acid and homoprotocatechuic\n\nacid.\n\nJournal\n\nof\n\nBacteriology\n\nlu), 159-167.\n\nSPARNINS,\n\nV.\n\nL.,\n\nANDERSON, J. J., OWNS, J.\n\n&\n\nDAGLEY,\n\nS.\n\n(1978).\n\nDegradation\n\nof\n\n4-hydroxyphenylacetic acid by\n\nTrichosporon cutan-\n\neum. Journal\n\nof\n\nBacteriology\n\n136,\n\n449-45\n\n1.\n\nSPOELSTRA,\n\nS.\n\nF. (1978). Degradation of tyrosine in anaerobically\n\nstored piggery wastes and in pig feces.\n\nApplied and Environmental\n\nMicrobiology\n\n36, 631-638.\n\nVAN\n\nDEN\n\nTWEEL, W. J. J., JANSSENS, R. J.\n\nJ.\n\n&\n\nDE\n\nBONT, J. A.\n\nM.\n\n(1 986).\n\nDegradation of 4-hydroxyphenylacetate by\n\nXanthobacter\n\n124X\n\n;\n\nphysiological resemblance with other Gram-negative\n\nbacteria.\n\nAntonie van Leeuwenhoek\n\n52, 309-3 18.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructural basis for cofactor and substrate selection by cyanobacterium succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1047847713000725)\nciteturn67search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Sanchez et al.\n\n### Purification and properties of two succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenases from Klebsiella pneumoniae ... ### Purification and properties of 2 succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenases from Klebsiella pneumoniae\n## Section snippets\n\n### Expression, purification and crystallization of Sp2771 and Gox0499 proteins\n\nThe full length Sp2771 and Gox0499 gene was PCR amplified from the genomic DNA of cyanobacterium Synechococcus and G. oxydans 621H purchased from ATCC.\n\nThe recombinant proteins were generated by the insertion of the PCR products into pET28b vector (Novagen; www.novagen.com) with an N-terminal His-6 tag. Mutant proteins were prepared using PCR-driven overlapping mutagenesis method and verified by sequencing. Recombinant Sp2771, Gox0499 and their mutants were expressed in E. coli (BL21/DE3 strain)\n\n### Crystal structure of Synechococcus SSADH\n\nTo confirm whether Sp2771 is a canonical SSADH and investigate the general catalytic mechanism by SSADH and the structural basis for cofactor and substrate selection by Sp2771, we determined the high resolution crystal structures of wild type Sp2771 and its S419A mutant at free form, and the ternary structure of C262A mutant in complex with NADP^{+} and SSA. Biophys.\n\n(1988)\n\n* M. Sanchez et al.\n\n### Purification and properties of two succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenases from Klebsiella pneumoniae\n\n### Biochim. Biophys. Acta\n\n(1989)\n\n* M. Sanchez et al.\n\n### Purification and properties of 2 succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenases from Klebsiella pneumoniae\n\n### Biochim. Biophys. Acta\n\n(1989)\n\n* S. Sheikh et al.\n\n### The potential roles of the conserved amino acids in human liver mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase\n\n### J. Biol. Chem.\n\n(1997)\n\n* N.A. Sophos et al.\n\n### Aldehyde dehydrogenase gene superfamily: the 2002 update\n\n### Chem. Biol. Interact.\n\n(2003)\n\n* C.G. Steinmetz et al.\n\n### Structure of mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase: the genetic component of ethanol aversion\n\n### Structure\n\n(1997)\n\n* Y. Tsybovsky et al.\n\n### Conserved catalytic residues of the ALDH1L1 aldehyde dehydrogenase domain control binding and discharging of the coenzyme\n\n### J. Biol. Chem.\n\n(2011)\n\n* M. Tylichova et al.\n\n### Structural and functional characterization of plant aminoaldehyde dehydrogenase from Pisum sativum with a broad specificity for natural and synthetic aminoaldehydes\n\n### J. Mol. Biol.\n\n(2010)\n\n* A.P. Wood et al.\n\n### A challenge for 21st century molecular biology and biochemistry: what are the causes of obligate autotrophy and methanotrophy?\n\n### FEMS Microbiol. Rev.\n\n(2004)\n\nView more references--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe X-Ray Crystal Structure of Escherichia coli Succinic Semialdehyde Dehydrogenase; Structural Insights into NADP+/Enzyme Interactions (https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pone.0009280)\nciteturn67search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Koh Y, Joo CN, Choi SY, Kim DS (1994) Purification and properties of succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase from Lumbricus rubellus. ... Sanchez M, Fernandez J, Martin M, Gibello A, Garrido-Pertierra A (1989) Purification and prop